### What is the "Tell me about yourself" question really asking?

This is not a request for your life story. The interviewer wants a professional narrative that answers three questions in under two minutes: what do you do today, what have you accomplished that proves you are good at it, and why are you sitting in this interview. Every second you spend on childhood, hobbies, or your full education history is a second wasted on something the interviewer did not ask for.

In code terms, think of yourself as a well-documented class that exposes only the relevant public API to the caller:

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public final class CandidateIntro {
    private final String currentRole;
    private final String keyAchievement;
    private final String futureGoal;

    public CandidateIntro(String currentRole, String keyAchievement, String futureGoal) {
        this.currentRole = currentRole;
        this.keyAchievement = keyAchievement;
        this.futureGoal = futureGoal;
    }

    public String present() { return currentRole; }
    public String past()    { return keyAchievement; }
    public String future()  { return futureGoal; }
}
```

You expose three clean methods: present, past, future. Nothing else leaks.

### Why does the present-past-future structure work?

The structure mirrors how the interviewer's brain processes you. First they need to place you: what level, what domain, what stack. Then they need proof: not a claim, but a result with a number. Finally they need motivation: why this role, why now. If you deliver all three in order, the interviewer walks away with a clear mental model of you.

A banking example of the present block:

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class PresentBlock {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String present = "I am a senior backend engineer on the payments platform "
            + "at National Bank of India, working in Java 17, Spring Boot, and Kafka.";
        System.out.println(present);
    }
}
```

```text
I am a senior backend engineer on the payments platform at National Bank of India, working in Java 17, Spring Boot, and Kafka.
```

### How do you build the past block with a measurable result?

The past block is your proof. Pick one achievement and state it as a result with a number. "I rebuilt our fund-transfer service and reduced settlement failures from three percent to under half a percent." The number is what makes a senior answer land, because it turns a claim into evidence.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class PastBlock {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(PastBlock.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double before = 3.0;
        double after = 0.4;
        log.info(() -> "Settlement failure rate improved: " + before + "% to " + after + "%");
    }
}
```

```text
Settlement failure rate improved: 3.0% to 0.4%
```

If you have two strong results, mention the second briefly, but one deep result beats two shallow ones.

### How do you build the future block that connects to this role?

The future block is the most commonly skipped part, and skipping it is a mistake. "I want to go deeper into real-time fraud detection, which is why this role interests me." This sentence does two things: it shows you have a direction, and it compliments the company by naming something specific they offer.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class FutureBlock {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String goal = "real-time fraud detection";
        String reason = "this role sits at the intersection of payments and fraud";
        System.out.println("Next goal: " + goal + " because " + reason);
    }
}
```

```text
Next goal: real-time fraud detection because this role sits at the intersection of payments and fraud
```

### How does STAR adapt to this question?

The STAR method compresses into one headline story. The Situation is your current company and team. The Task is your role and what you own. The Action is the one signature thing you built or improved. The Result is the measurable number.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class StarIntro {
    record STAR(String situation, String task, String action, String result) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        STAR intro = new STAR(
            "Payments platform at National Bank of India",
            "Own the fund-transfer service",
            "Rebuilt retry and reconciliation flow with idempotency",
            "Failure rate 3% to 0.4%"
        );
        System.out.println(intro);
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes in this answer?

The most common mistake is the resume read-out: starting from college, listing every company, every project, every technology. The interviewer zones out by minute three. The second mistake is being too humble and giving no result. The third is being too generic: "I am a passionate engineer who loves technology" could be said by anyone.

Wrong approach:

```java
// Wrong: resume read-out, no structure, no number
String bad = "I graduated from XYZ in 2015, then I joined ABC company "
    + "where I worked on many projects, then I moved to DEF "
    + "and did a lot of backend work. I like coding.";
```

Correct approach:

```java
// Correct: present, past (with number), future (with direction)
String good = "I am a senior backend engineer at National Bank of India "
    + "owning the fund-transfer service in Java and Spring Boot. "
    + "I rebuilt the transfer pipeline and cut failures from 3% to 0.4%. "
    + "I want to go deeper into real-time fraud, which is why this role excites me.";
```

### How do you tailor the answer to different roles?

If the role is payments-focused, lead with a payments achievement. If the role is data engineering, lead with a data pipeline result. The core structure stays the same; only the content shifts. Think of it as parameterizing your story:

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class TailoredIntro {
    public static String build(String roleType) {
        return switch (roleType) {
            case "payments"  -> "Cut settlement failures from 3% to 0.4%";
            case "fraud"     -> "Built real-time fraud scoring at 50ms p99";
            case "data"      -> "Reduced ETL pipeline runtime from 6h to 45min";
            default          -> "Improved system reliability measurably";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Lead achievement: " + build("payments"));
    }
}
```

```text
Lead achievement: Cut settlement failures from 3% to 0.4%
```

### What should a senior engineer emphasize?

A senior engineer emphasizes ownership and impact, not just coding. You own a service or a platform, not just tasks. You drive technical decisions. You mentor others. And you quantify business impact, not just technical output. "I reduced settlement failures" is business impact; "I refactored the retry module" is technical output. The interviewer remembers the business impact.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class SeniorEmphasis {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(SeniorEmphasis.class.getName());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String ownership = "Own the fund-transfer service end to end";
        String impact = "Cut customer complaints by 60% through reliability improvements";
        String mentoring = "Mentored 2 junior engineers on transactional correctness";
        log.info(() -> ownership + " | " + impact + " | " + mentoring);
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

After "Tell me about yourself", expect these follow-ups:
- "Tell me more about that failure-rate improvement" : be ready to go deeper into the STAR of your lead achievement.
- "Why are you leaving your current company?" : covered in Q2471.
- "Where do you see yourself in five years?" : covered in Q2472.
- "What is your biggest strength?" : covered in Q2473.

Prepare a five-minute deep-dive version of the same achievement, because the interviewer will likely pull on the thread you dangled.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class FollowUpReady {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String twoMinVersion = "Cut failures from 3% to 0.4%";
        String fiveMinVersion = twoMinVersion
            + " by adding idempotency keys, fixing transaction boundaries, "
            + "and building a reconciliation job that auto-heals stuck transfers.";
        System.out.println("Short: " + twoMinVersion);
        System.out.println("Deep:  " + fiveMinVersion);
    }
}
```

```text
Short: Cut failures from 3% to 0.4%
Deep:  Cut failures from 3% to 0.4% by adding idempotency keys, fixing transaction boundaries, and building a reconciliation job that auto-heals stuck transfers.
```
